<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leonard Woolley</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1880">1880</a>–<a href="/years/1960">1960</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir Leonard Woolley, Charles Leonard Woolley</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Leonard Woolley was a British archaeologist best known for excavating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> from 1922 to 1934. There he uncovered the Royal Cemetery, with the tomb of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puabi">Puabi</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/standard-of-ur">Standard of Ur</a> and the golden helmet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meskalamdug">Meskalamdug</a>. He later excavated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alalakh">Alalakh</a>, capital of the Kingdom of Mukish, at Tell Atchana from 1937, recovering palaces and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> archives.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leonard-woolley.jpg" alt="Leonard Woolley" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hulton-Deutsch Collection · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leonard_Woolley_Getty.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles Leonard Woolley was born on 17 April 1880 at Upper Clapton, in the modern London Borough of Hackney, the son of a clergyman. He was educated at St John's School, Leatherhead, and New College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, and was interested in excavation from a young age. In 1905 he became assistant at the Ashmolean Museum, Oxford, and in 1906 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-evans">Arthur Evans</a> volunteered him to run the excavations at the Roman site of Corbridge, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a>. He later admitted in his book Spadework that he had no idea how to make a survey or a ground-plan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Woolley then worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> with David Randall-MacIver, between 1907 and 1911. In 1912 to 1914 he excavated the Hittite city of Carchemish in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/t-e-lawrence">T. E. Lawrence</a> as his assistant. The two were apparently working for British Naval Intelligence, monitoring the building of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s Berlin to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> railway. In the First World War Woolley worked on naval espionage from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>. His requisitioned yacht Zaida sank off Alexandretta on 17 August 1916, after striking a French-laid mine, and he was held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> as a prisoner of war for two years.</p><p>From 1922 he led a joint expedition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> and the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, which included his wife, the archaeologist Katharine Woolley. Among the finds in the royal cemetery were the Bull-Headed Lyre and the pair of Ram in a Thicket figurines. Queen Pu-abi's tomb was untouched by looters. Her body lay with two attendants who had been killed to serve her after death, and Woolley reconstructed her funeral from the objects. Agatha Christie, invited to a dig in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> in 1930, met her second husband, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/max-mallowan">Max Mallowan</a>, there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Interested in links between the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, Woolley turned in 1936 to Al Mina in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and excavated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alalakh">Tell Atchana</a> in 1937 to 1939 and 1946 to 1949. His team found palaces, temples, houses and fortification walls in 17 levels, including the inscribed statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idrimi">Idrimi</a>, a king of Alalakh in the early 15th century BCE. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> he identified a flood stratum, and was among the first to propose that the flood of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-genesis">Book of Genesis</a> was local.</p><p>He was knighted in 1935 and is regarded as one of the first modern archaeologists to excavate methodically, keeping careful records and using them to reconstruct ancient life. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> he served in the Monuments, Fine Arts and Archives Section of the Allied armies. His books include The Sumerians (1928) and Excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a> (1954). He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 20 February 1960.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Leonard Woolley” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Ubaid period takes its name from Tell al-ʿUbaid in southern Iraq, where the first large excavation of its material was carried out by Henry Hall in 1919, <b>Leonard Woolley</b> in 1922–1923 and Pinhas Delougaz in 1937.</blockquote><a href="/culture/ubaid-culture">Ubaid culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>British Museum excavations from 1911, with <b>Leonard Woolley</b> and T. E. Lawrence, uncovered much of the city.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-carchemish">Kingdom of Carchemish</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Leonard Woolley's</b> excavations from 1937 recovered palaces and archives from this period.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-mukish">Kingdom of Mukish</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Upper Clapton, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.57,-0.06&amp;year=1932" aria-label="Upper Clapton on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Leonard Woolley"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4997 5718h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4997 5718h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.57,-0.06&amp;year=1932">What was here in 1932? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
